Nail fungus does not discriminate based on how often you wash your feet. That is the first thing most articles on this subject get wrong, and it shapes every misunderstanding that follows.

<strong>Nail fungus causes</strong> are not rooted in poor hygiene. The clinical term is onychomycosis, and <strong>nail fungus causes</strong> begin with microscopic fungi that live in the environment  on surfaces, in soil, on skin  entering the nail through a gap, a crack, or a small separation between nail and skin. Once inside, they feed on keratin, the protein that gives your nails their hard structure, and proliferate in the warm, enclosed conditions that nails happen to provide naturally. Understanding this changes how you think about your own situation. It is not about what you failed to do. It is about what the biology made possible.

Roughly 3 to 12% of the general population has onychomycosis at any given time, according to <a href='https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279547/'>clinical research on nail fungus</a>. Among people over 70, that figure rises to approximately 50%. The infection is common, it is frequently misunderstood, and for many people, it keeps coming back  not because they are doing something wrong, but because the underlying conditions that allowed it in the first place have not been addressed.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## How Nail Fungus Actually Gets Into Your Nail

One millimetre. That is all the gap required. A tiny separation between the nail plate and the nail bed, a small crack in the skin at the corner of the nail, a micro-abrasion along the cuticle line  any of these is enough for fungal spores to gain entry.

The nail itself is not a sealed structure. It sits in contact with the skin of the nail bed, separated from it by a thin tissue called the hyponychium. This junction is remarkably susceptible to minor disruptions. Repeated friction from footwear, a nail that grows slightly unevenly, the small drying and cracking that comes with age  all of these create the microscopic entry points that fungi need. Once spores penetrate past the nail plate into the subungual space beneath it, they are effectively protected from topical treatment and from the body's usual immune surveillance.

The nail bed is a warm, dark, often moist environment. Fungi do not seek it out intentionally; they colonise it opportunistically. The same spores that land harmlessly on intact, well-sealed skin find a foothold the moment the surface is compromised. This is why nail trauma, however minor, is one of the most consistent precursors to infection. It is also why toenails are infected far more often than fingernails  they spend their days compressed in shoes, subject to repeated micro-impacts, with far less blood flow to help the immune system respond.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## The Three Types of Fungi That Cause Nail Infections

Not all nail fungus has the same origin. The organism matters, because different fungi behave differently, present differently, and in some cases respond to different treatments.

A dermatophyte is a type of fungi that feeds on keratin  the protein that makes up the hard surface of your nails  and accounts for approximately 90% of all nail fungus cases. Trichophyton rubrum is the most common single organism responsible for nail fungal infections worldwide. It is also the organism responsible for athlete's foot and ringworm. Dermatophyte-driven nail infections, clinically referred to as tinea unguium, typically begin at the free edge of the nail and progress inward, causing the nail to thicken, discolour, and crumble over time.

Yeasts, most commonly Candida species, account for a smaller proportion of nail infections  more common on fingernails than toenails, and more frequently seen in people with compromised immune systems or those who keep their hands in water for extended periods. Mould infections are the least common category and tend to occur in older adults or immunosuppressed individuals, often affecting nails that have already been damaged by trauma or prior infection.

For a full overview of the <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/what-is-nail-fungus'>types of nail fungus</a> and how each presents clinically, the pillar guide covers this in detail. What matters here is the starting point: in the vast majority of cases, you are dealing with a dermatophyte, and the organism almost certainly entered through a break in the nail or surrounding skin.

